WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.886
What if evolution could design a neural network for you?

00:00:03.886 --> 00:00:06.649
Not just its weights, but its whole shape.

00:00:06.649 --> 00:00:09.767
This is NEAT, learning to land on the Moon.

00:00:10.367 --> 00:00:12.143
This is Lunar Lander.

00:00:12.143 --> 00:00:16.003
The goal is to touch down softly between the two flags.

00:00:16.003 --> 00:00:21.142
Gravity pulls the lander down, and every burst of an engine costs fuel.

00:00:21.742 --> 00:00:25.148
At every step, the lander senses eight numbers.

00:00:25.148 --> 00:00:32.569
Where it is, how fast it is moving, how it is tilted and spinning, and whether each leg touches the ground.

00:00:33.169 --> 00:00:36.849
And at every step, it chooses one of four actions.

00:00:36.849 --> 00:00:42.795
Do nothing, fire the left engine, fire the main engine, or fire the right engine.

00:00:43.395 --> 00:00:46.050
The game hands out points as it goes.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:50.415
Moving toward the pad, slowing down and staying level earn points.

00:00:50.415 --> 00:00:54.196
Drifting away, speeding up and tilting cost points.

00:00:54.796 --> 00:00:57.005
There are also one-off events.

00:00:57.005 --> 00:00:59.969
Each leg that touches the ground earns ten.

00:00:59.969 --> 00:01:02.236
Coming to rest earns a hundred.

00:01:02.236 --> 00:01:04.396
A crash costs a hundred.

00:01:04.996 --> 00:01:06.769
And fuel isn't free.

00:01:06.769 --> 00:01:10.677
Every frame the main engine fires costs a little.

00:01:11.277 --> 00:01:15.495
An average of two hundred points counts as solved.

00:01:16.095 --> 00:01:18.651
The pilot is a small neural network.

00:01:18.651 --> 00:01:21.265
The eight inputs come in on the left.

00:01:21.265 --> 00:01:27.549
They flow along weighted connections, and come out on the right as four scores, one per action.

00:01:28.149 --> 00:01:30.983
The action with the highest score fires.

00:01:30.983 --> 00:01:34.353
Watch the scores change as the lander flies.

00:01:34.953 --> 00:01:40.961
This network has no hidden nodes at all, just seventeen of the thirty-two possible connections.

00:01:40.961 --> 00:01:42.536
Nobody designed it.

00:01:42.536 --> 00:01:47.269
Evolution found every weight, and decided which connections to keep.

00:01:47.869 --> 00:01:50.972
Evolution doesn't work on the network directly.

00:01:50.972 --> 00:01:53.568
It works on a genome: a list of genes.

00:01:53.568 --> 00:01:55.994
Node genes say which neurons exist.

00:01:55.994 --> 00:02:00.734
Connection genes say which neurons are wired together, and with what weight.

00:02:00.734 --> 00:02:04.025
Read the list, and you can build the network.

00:02:04.625 --> 00:02:09.870
Each connection gene also carries a historical marker, called its innovation number.

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:15.002
It is given out the first time that connection appears anywhere in the population.

00:02:15.002 --> 00:02:19.392
So when two genomes share a number, those genes have the same origin.

00:02:19.392 --> 00:02:25.222
The library we use names each gene by the two nodes it connects, which does the same job.

00:02:25.822 --> 00:02:30.255
Each new genome starts as a copy of a parent, and then it mutates.

00:02:30.255 --> 00:02:33.071
There are three kinds of mutation.

00:02:33.671 --> 00:02:36.835
The most common one simply nudges a weight.

00:02:36.835 --> 00:02:40.423
Here, a positive connection becomes negative.

00:02:41.023 --> 00:02:46.485
The second kind adds a connection, joining two nodes that were not connected before.

00:02:46.485 --> 00:02:49.160
It gets a new innovation number.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:51.687
The third kind adds a node.

00:02:51.687 --> 00:02:54.329
It splits an existing connection in two.

00:02:54.329 --> 00:02:58.238
The old gene is switched off, and two new genes take its place.

00:02:58.238 --> 00:03:06.496
The connection into the new node gets a weight of one, and the one out keeps the old weight, so the network behaves almost the same as before.

00:03:06.496 --> 00:03:10.044
The structure has grown, without breaking anything.

00:03:10.644 --> 00:03:12.968
Two parents can also be combined.

00:03:12.968 --> 00:03:15.973
This is where the innovation numbers pay off.

00:03:15.973 --> 00:03:21.602
Line the genes up by their numbers, and you can see which genes the two parents share.

00:03:22.202 --> 00:03:25.311
Genes that both parents have are called matching.

00:03:25.311 --> 00:03:29.457
A gene missing in the middle of the other parent is called disjoint.

00:03:29.457 --> 00:03:33.630
And a gene beyond the end of the other parent is called excess.

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:38.381
The child takes each matching gene from either parent at random.

00:03:38.381 --> 00:03:42.186
The disjoint and excess genes come from the fitter parent.

00:03:42.186 --> 00:03:47.905
No matter how different the two networks look, the child is always a sensible network.

00:03:48.505 --> 00:03:49.975
Here is a problem.

00:03:49.975 --> 00:03:56.760
A mutation that adds a node usually makes the network worse at first, because its new weights are not tuned yet.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:02.206
Ranked against everyone, it would be thrown out before it could ever become useful.

00:04:02.806 --> 00:04:06.015
So NEAT measures how different two genomes are.

00:04:06.015 --> 00:04:13.834
It counts the genes that don't match, adds the weight differences of the genes that do, and divides by the size of the genome.

00:04:13.834 --> 00:04:19.093
If that distance is small enough, the two genomes belong to the same species.

00:04:19.693 --> 00:04:23.392
Genomes then compete mostly inside their own species.

00:04:23.392 --> 00:04:30.061
Each species shares its offspring among its members, so a big species can't crowd out a small new one.

00:04:30.061 --> 00:04:34.909
And a species that stops improving for twenty generations dies out.

00:04:35.509 --> 00:04:38.449
So here is one generation of NEAT.

00:04:38.449 --> 00:04:44.769
First, evaluate: every one of the 150 genomes flies, and gets a fitness score.

00:04:45.369 --> 00:04:49.640
Then, speciate: sort the genomes into species.

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:58.324
Then, select: inside each species, the best ones become parents, and the two very best are kept unchanged.

00:04:58.924 --> 00:05:03.657
And reproduce: crossover and mutation fill the population back up.

00:05:03.657 --> 00:05:06.068
Then it all starts again.

00:05:06.668 --> 00:05:10.991
Fitness is the average reward over five flights.

00:05:11.591 --> 00:05:18.012
Every genome in a generation flies the same starting positions, so the comparison is fair.

00:05:18.012 --> 00:05:23.881
The starting positions change each generation, so no genome can memorise one.

00:05:24.481 --> 00:05:29.067
But five flights are noisy, and the top genome is often just lucky.

00:05:29.067 --> 00:05:35.244
So each generation, its champion also flies a hundred flights that no genome ever trained on.

00:05:35.244 --> 00:05:40.742
We only call the problem solved when that unseen average reaches two hundred.

00:05:41.342 --> 00:05:42.848
Now the real run.

00:05:42.848 --> 00:05:46.764
Every dot is one genome, and its color shows its species.

00:05:46.764 --> 00:05:53.512
In generation zero, every network is just the inputs wired straight to the outputs, with random weights.

00:05:53.512 --> 00:05:55.800
Almost all of them crash.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:00.589
Generation by generation, the cloud of dots creeps upward.

00:06:00.589 --> 00:06:03.492
New species appear in new colors.

00:06:04.092 --> 00:06:08.247
The green line is the champion on the hundred unseen flights.

00:06:08.247 --> 00:06:12.041
It first passes two hundred in generation thirty-seven.

00:06:12.041 --> 00:06:17.218
The best champion of all came from generation one hundred and sixty-four.

00:06:17.818 --> 00:06:20.206
This is the classic NEAT picture.

00:06:20.206 --> 00:06:24.632
Each band is one species, and its height is how many genomes it has.

00:06:24.632 --> 00:06:27.261
At the start, there is just one.

00:06:27.861 --> 00:06:33.592
New species split off, grow when they do well, and shrink and die when they stop improving.

00:06:33.592 --> 00:06:37.539
Over the whole run, twenty-seven species came and went.

00:06:38.139 --> 00:06:41.201
Here is one generation flying together.

00:06:41.201 --> 00:06:46.738
All 150 genomes start from the same spot, and we draw each flight as a trail.

00:06:46.738 --> 00:06:49.123
This is generation zero.

00:06:49.723 --> 00:06:53.889
At first, nearly every flight ends in a crash.

00:06:56.323 --> 00:07:01.847
Fifty generations in, a few dozen trails curve down onto the pad.

00:07:02.923 --> 00:07:10.758
And late in the run, about a third of the population lands from this start, and their trails bundle together into one shared path.

00:07:10.758 --> 00:07:12.405
The rest still crash.

00:07:12.405 --> 00:07:16.963
Mutation keeps breaking things, and that is the price of searching.

00:07:17.563 --> 00:07:20.731
This is how the champion's network changed.

00:07:20.731 --> 00:07:27.554
It started fully connected, with all eight inputs wired to all four outputs, and no hidden nodes.

00:07:28.154 --> 00:07:33.030
For its first hundred and sixty generations, evolution mostly removed connections.

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:41.537
The winner, from generation one hundred and sixty-four, has no hidden nodes at all, and just seventeen of the thirty-two connections it started with.

00:07:41.537 --> 00:07:46.468
Later, the population grew much bigger networks, with fifteen or more hidden nodes.

00:07:46.468 --> 00:07:49.664
But those flew worse on unseen flights, not better.

00:07:49.664 --> 00:07:55.491
A bigger network is not automatically a better one, and that is exactly why NEAT starts small.

00:07:56.091 --> 00:08:01.668
Here are the champions from different generations, all flying the same start.

00:08:02.268 --> 00:08:04.992
The early ones crash or drift away.

00:08:04.992 --> 00:08:10.170
Later champions slow down, straighten up, and land between the flags.

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:12.849
So what did evolution build?

00:08:12.849 --> 00:08:17.653
Let's switch connections off one at a time, and see which ones matter.

00:08:18.253 --> 00:08:24.734
The most important connection runs from the falling speed to the main engine, with a big negative weight.

00:08:24.734 --> 00:08:31.616
When the lander falls fast, its vertical speed is negative, so the main engine's score shoots up, and it brakes.

00:08:31.616 --> 00:08:36.160
Switch this one connection off, and the lander never lands at all.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:39.794
Next, the tilt angle feeds the left engine.

00:08:39.794 --> 00:08:43.422
This is the connection that keeps the lander upright.

00:08:43.422 --> 00:08:46.934
Without it, only five percent of flights land.

00:08:47.534 --> 00:08:49.298
And here is a surprise.

00:08:49.298 --> 00:08:55.729
Once the right leg touches the ground, its sensor tells the engines to stop, and votes for doing nothing.

00:08:55.729 --> 00:09:01.249
Without that, the lander keeps nudging itself and lands only fifteen percent of the time.

00:09:01.249 --> 00:09:04.492
Evolution ignored the left leg sensor completely.

00:09:04.492 --> 00:09:06.329
One leg was enough.

00:09:06.929 --> 00:09:13.525
Here are the connections that matter most, ranked by how much the landing rate drops without them.

00:09:14.125 --> 00:09:18.284
Nine of the seventeen connections can go with almost no loss.

00:09:18.284 --> 00:09:21.478
A handful of them carry the whole skill.

00:09:22.078 --> 00:09:30.139
On a hundred new flights, the winner lands ninety-five percent of the time, with an average reward of two hundred and fifty-four.

00:09:30.139 --> 00:09:34.734
And it does that with no hidden nodes, and seventeen connections.

00:09:35.334 --> 00:09:39.392
The code, the training run, and these videos are all on GitHub.

00:09:39.392 --> 00:09:43.236
Clone it, train your own, and watch your network grow.
